@@ -381,6 +381,11 @@ job.  That includes the cursor position.  Typed keys are sent to the job.
 The terminal contents can change at any time.  This is called Terminal-Job
 mode.
 
+In Terminal-Job mode the cursor position you see belongs to the terminal job,
+not to Vim.  The window's cursor position is only updated when Vim takes over
+in |Terminal-Normal| mode, so the 'ruler' and the 'statusline' show a stale
+cursor position.
+
 Use CTRL-W N (or 'termwinkey' N) to switch to Terminal-Normal mode.  Now the
 contents of the terminal window is under control of Vim, the job output is
 suspended.  CTRL-\ CTRL-N does the same.
